ol is technically a pool of  crypto tokens secured under a smart  contract and these tokens provide  decentralized exchanges with the  necessary liquidity they require  basically the term liquidity refers to  the ease with which one can swap a  crypto token for another because so many  Financial transactions occur within the  D5 ecosystem such Simplicity is crucial  but this raises the question of how  liquidity pulls differ from ordered  books  to understand how liquidity pools differ  from ordered books let's look at the  fundamental building block of electronic  trading which is the ordered book  simply put the open orders for a certain  Market are gathered in the ordered book  the system that matches orders with each  other is called the matching engine the  ordered book and the matching engine are  the primary components of any  centralized exchange or CeX  this concept made it possible to  communicate information efficiently and  enabled the development of intricate  financial markets defy trading however  carries out deals directly on the  blockchain without needing a central  entity to retain the funds in terms of  ordered books this creates a challenge  gas fees are incurred for each  interaction with the ordered book making  the cost of executing transactions  significantly higher additionally it  makes it very expensive for Traders who  act as market makers and Supply  liquidity for trading payers Above All  Else however most blockchains cannot  handle the throughput needed for the  daily trading of billions of dollars but  liquidity pools solve each of these  problems with ease so now that the  definition and purpose of the liquidity  pool are all clear let me walk you  through the working of liquidity pools  as discussed before in a normal  liquidity pool users are encouraged and  rewarded for staking their digital  Assets in the pool rewards may take the  form of cryptocurrency or a portion of  the trading commissions paid by the  exchanges where they 